---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---
<DocsLayout title="Loading & saving — dotcfg" description="Choose how missing configuration behaves and persist complete typed structs.">
  <article class="prose">
    <span class="eyebrow">Core API</span><h1>Loading & saving</h1>
    <p class="lead">dotcfg separates “there is no config yet” from “the config is broken.” You decide whether a missing file is normal, an error, or a reason to create defaults.</p>

    <h2>Optional: <code>load()</code></h2>
    <p>Use this when your application can run without a saved config. A missing file becomes <code>Ok(None)</code>; I/O and parse failures still return errors.</p>
    <div class="code-window"><div class="code-header"><span>Rust</span></div><pre><code>let config: Option&lt;AppConfig&gt; = cfg.load()?;

if let Some(config) = config {
    println!("Loaded {}", config.theme);
}</code></pre></div>

    <h2>Required: <code>load_or_error()</code></h2>
    <p>Use this when the application must be configured before it can continue.</p>
    <div class="code-window"><div class="code-header"><span>Rust</span></div><pre><code>let config: AppConfig = cfg.load_or_error()?;</code></pre></div>

    <h2>Create defaults: <code>load_or_default()</code></h2>
    <p>This requires your type to implement <code>Default</code>. If the file is missing, dotcfg constructs <code>T::default()</code>, saves it, and returns that value.</p>
    <div class="code-window"><div class="code-header"><span>Rust</span></div><pre><code>#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Default)]
struct AppConfig {
    theme: String,
}

let config: AppConfig = cfg.load_or_default()?;</code></pre></div>

    <h2>Save a complete config</h2>
    <p><code>save()</code> serializes your struct in the selected format. The config directory is created automatically when it does not exist.</p>
    <div class="code-window"><div class="code-header"><span>Rust</span></div><pre><code>let config = AppConfig {
    theme: "dark".into(),
};

cfg.save(&config)?;</code></pre></div>

    <div class="note"><strong>Whole config vs one key</strong><p>Use <code>save()</code> when your program already has the complete config struct. If you only need to change one field while preserving everything else, use <a class="inline-link" href="/key-access"><code>set()</code> or <code>set_val()</code></a>.</p></div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
